<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Quanzhou</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Quanzhou is a port city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a> province in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty</a> it was one of the busiest ports in the world, linking China with South-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia">Asia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>. Customs duties from its trade were a significant source of Song revenue.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/quanzhou.jpg" alt="Quanzhou" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Windmemories · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20230130_Old_City_of_Quanzhou_01.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Quanzhou lies on a strip of land between the estuaries of the Jin River and the Luoyang River, where they flow into Quanzhou Bay on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan-strait">Taiwan Strait</a>. Foreign traders knew it as Zaiton or Zayton, from the Arabic name meaning City of Olives, which is traditionally linked to its Chinese epithet, the Coral-tree City, after the coral trees said to have been planted around it by its 10th-century ruler Liu Congxiao.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Quanzhou proper was established under the Tang in 718, and Muslim traders reached it early. After the fall of the Min state, the Qingyuan Circuit, with Quanzhou as its capital, was de facto independent from 949 to 978 under Liu Congxiao, who promoted overseas trade. In the first century of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Northern Song</a> it became the starting point of the Maritime Silk Road, eclipsing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangzhou">Guangzhou</a>. A 1095 inscription records two convoys of twenty ships each arriving from the Southern Seas every year, and celadon made for export accounted for ninety percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fujian">Fujian</a>'s ceramic production. A report of 1206 lists merchants from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sumatra">Sumatra</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>, and Tamil merchants built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> temples there.</p><p>Under the Yuan, a superintendent of foreign trade was established in 1277. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marco-polo">Marco Polo</a> recorded a 10 percent duty on the port's commerce and described it as one of the two chief havens for commerce in the world; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a> ranked it first among the world's ports. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kublai-khan">Kublai Khan</a>'s invasions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> sailed mainly from it. From 1357 a revolt by the Persian garrison against anti-Muslim laws lasted a decade and was followed by massacres of foreign communities. Under the Ming, foreign commerce was discouraged; piracy forced the Superintendency of Trade to close in 1522, and silting of the harbour later hindered larger ships.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Quanzhou is a prefecture-level city with a population of 8,782,285 at the 2020 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. In 2021 Quanzhou: Emporium of the World in Song-Yuan China was inscribed on the UNESCO World Heritage List. The Qingjing Mosque, which dates from 1009, is now a museum, and the Kaiyuan Temple has two five-storey pagodas of the 13th century. About 6 million overseas Chinese, most in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, trace their ancestry to the Quanzhou area.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Quanzhou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Maritime trade through Guangzhou, <b>Quanzhou</b> and Mingzhou brought in large customs revenues, collected by maritime trade superintendencies, and connected Song China to Southeast Asia, South Asia, the Persian Gulf and East Africa.</blockquote><a href="/empire/song-dynasty">Song Dynasty · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Southern Song government sponsored massive shipbuilding and harbour improvement projects, and built beacons and seaport warehouses to support trade abroad through the major international seaports of <b>Quanzhou</b>, Guangzhou and Xiamen.</blockquote><a href="/empire/southern-song">Southern Song · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>A temple at Cao’an near <b>Quanzhou</b> in southern China still holds a stone image of Mani as the “Buddha of Light”, carved in 1339: the only Manichaean shrine in the world known to survive.</blockquote><a href="/religion/manichaeism">Manichaeism · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>…capital of Ghana; along the monsoon routes Islam became the religion of the Swahili towns of East Africa; Muslim merchants lived in Guangzhou and <b>Quanzhou</b>; and in northern Sumatra the gravestone of Sultan Malik al-Salih, of 1297, is the earliest evidence of a Muslim ruler in Southeast Asia.</blockquote><a href="/essays/faiths-on-the-move">Faiths on the Move</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Southern Song clansmen took the examinations and served as officials like other scholars, and many settled in Fujian, at Fuzhou and <b>Quanzhou</b>.</blockquote><a href="/societies/song-china">Social Hierarchy of Song China</a></li><li><blockquote>After the conquest of the Song, Muslim, Uyghur and Chinese merchants sailed from the southern ports such as <b>Quanzhou</b> to the South China Sea and the Indian Ocean, and from 1286 maritime trade was taxed through the Office of Market Taxes.</blockquote><a href="/societies/yuan-china">Social Hierarchy of Yuan China</a></li></ul></section>
